### What are the three biggest pitfalls?

Blaming others, hiding behind 'we', and giving vague answers without numbers. These are the most common ways candidates weaken an otherwise good story.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
public class ThreePitfalls {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> pitfalls = List.of(
            "1. Blaming others: 'It was the PM's fault'",
            "2. Hiding behind 'we': 'We fixed the issue'",
            "3. Vagueness: 'Things improved'"
        );
        pitfalls.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### Why is blaming dangerous?

Even when a problem was genuinely caused by someone else, an answer that blames signals poor teamwork and low accountability. The interviewer wonders: will they blame us too?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class NoBlame {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Blame: 'The deadline was unrealistic and the PM set us up to fail.'");
        System.out.println("Ownership: 'The deadline was tight. I raised the risk early and re-scoped "
            + "to deliver the critical path on time.'");
        System.out.println("Same situation, completely different impression.");
    }
}
```

### How do you replace 'we' with 'I'?

Keep your personal actions visible. You can credit the team, but the interviewer must be able to see what you specifically contributed.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class IvsWeFix {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String weak = "We decided to use async. We built it. We deployed it.";
        String strong = "I proposed async based on load test data. "
            + "I built the event publishing layer. "
            + "The team built the consumer, and I coordinated the deployment.";
        System.out.println("Weak:   " + weak);
        System.out.println("Strong: " + strong);
    }
}
```

### How do you replace vagueness with numbers?

'It got faster' becomes 'p99 dropped from 2000ms to 180ms.' 'Errors went down' becomes 'failure rate from 3% to 0.4%.' The number is the proof.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class VagueToSpecific {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(VagueToSpecific.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Vague: 'Transfers got faster'");
        log.info("Specific: 'p99: 2000ms -> 180ms (91% improvement)'");
        log.info("Vague: 'Errors went down'");
        log.info("Specific: 'Failure rate: 3.0% -> 0.4% (-87%)'");
    }
}
```

```text
Vague: 'Transfers got faster'
Specific: 'p99: 2000ms -> 180ms (91% improvement)'
Vague: 'Errors went down'
Specific: 'Failure rate: 3.0% -> 0.4% (-87%)'
```

### Why do these pitfalls compound?

A vague, team-voiced, blame-shifting answer is the worst possible combination. The fixes also compound: ownership + first person + numbers = a strong, confident story.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class CompoundingEffect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Worst: blame + we + vague = sounds like someone who was merely present");
        System.out.println("Best: own + I + number = sounds like someone who delivers results");
    }
}
```

### How do you detect these in your own practice?

Review your stories and count 'we' vs 'I'. Check every Result for a number. Scan for any sentence that assigns blame to another person.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class SelfCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String story = "I proposed async. I built the publisher. p99: 2000ms -> 180ms.";
        long weCount = story.chars().filter(c -> false).count(); // simplified
        long iCount = story.split("\\bI\\b").length - 1;
        boolean hasNumber = story.contains("ms") || story.contains("%");
        System.out.println("'I' count: " + iCount + " (should be > 0)");
        System.out.println("Has number: " + hasNumber + " (must be true)");
        System.out.println("No blame language: check manually");
    }
}
```

### What does a strong answer look like end to end?

A complete example that avoids all three pitfalls and passes all checks.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class StrongExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("S: Transfers at National Bank of India had a 3% failure rate");
        System.out.println("T: I owned the reliability of the transfer service");
        System.out.println("A: I traced the failures to missing idempotency, I added request-id keys, "
            + "I built a reconciliation job");
        System.out.println("R: Failure rate dropped from 3% to 0.4% (-87%)");
        System.out.println("No blame. First person. Measurable result.");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class AvoidFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("What was the team's role? -> Credit them, keep your actions visible");
        System.out.println("Was anyone else responsible? -> Focus on what I did, assume good intent");
        System.out.println("Can you be more specific? -> Always have the number ready");
    }
}
```
